{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QSgQhq4ce2xbW1ge3x4u7L","type":"tool_result","content":"/ux/set/bin/metais\nmetais 0.0.0-devel\nspecification 1.0\nAccessibility conformance report — accessibility-conformance-report 1.0.0\nAn accessibility conformance report (ACR) for one product, as a Word document and a JSON copy, from the product's details and one CSV table of criteria for each standard its edition answers.\nMETAIS 1.0, packed, sha256:1d29c7f0c90d4a4ade57ba4cc3ab68e430081dd04bfd15af32c8ba34f889b157\n\nGuide\nThis package writes an accessibility conformance report (ACR): a\ndocument that tells a buyer how far one product meets the accessibility\nstandards they ask about. It writes two files:\n\n- **acr.docx**, the report as a Word document: the product, its vendor,\n  how it was evaluated, the standards it answers, what each conformance\n  level means, and one table per standard with a row per criterion.\n  The WCAG 2.2 table carries W3C's notice for the criterion names and\n  levels it copies, as references/sources.md says.\n- **acr.json**, the same report as JSON for programs, in the shape\n  references/acr.schema.json gives, W3C's notice included. Every render\n  checks the file against that schema before it writes anything.\n\nGather the inputs in this order, as files of one data directory:\n\n1. **report** (report.yaml or report.json): the product, the vendor,\n   the report's date, how the product was evaluated, and the\n   **edition**. Read [what the report details mean](inputs/report.md)\n   first: the edition decides which tables you supply.\n2. **wcag** (wcag.csv): the WCAG 2.2 table, which every edition holds.\n3. **section508** (section508.csv): the Revised Section 508 table, for\n   the section508 and international editions only.\n4. **en301549** (en301549.csv): the EN 301 549 V4.1.1 table, for the en301549\n   and international editions only.\n\nEach table is a CSV file with the header `criterion,conformance,remarks`\nand a row per criterion. [How to fill in a table](inputs/tables.md) says\nwhat each column holds, which criteria to list and how to write the\nremarks, and [what the conformance levels mean](inputs/conformance.md)\nsays which level to choose. What each criterion asks is in the\npackage's references: references/wcag-2.2.md, references/section-508.md\nand references/en-301-549.md, one section per criterion. Search them\nwith `metais grep <package> <number or word> references/`.\n\nBase every level and remark on an evaluation of the real product, and\nsay how it was evaluated in the report's methods. A criterion you did\nnot evaluate is Not Evaluated, never Supports.\n\nThen run `metais validate <package> --data <dir>`, which reports every\nproblem at once: a value that does not fit, a criterion number that does\nnot exist, or a table the edition needs and you left out, or one it\ndoes not hold and you supplied. Fix them all, and run\n`metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>` to write the report.\n\nInputs\n  report — Report details (required; report.yaml, report.yml, report.json)\n    The product, its vendor, the report's date and edition, and how the product was evaluated.\n  wcag — WCAG 2.2 table (required; wcag.csv)\n    One row per WCAG 2.2 success criterion: its number, the product's conformance level and remarks. Every edition holds it.\n  section508 — Revised Section 508 table (optional; section508.csv)\n    One row per criterion of chapters 3 to 6 of the Revised Section 508 Standards. Supplied for the section508 and international editions, and for no other.\n  en301549 — EN 301 549 table (optional; en301549.csv)\n    One row per clause of EN 301 549 V4.1.1 outside its WCAG-based clauses. Supplied for the en301549 and international editions, and for no other.\n\nOutputs\n  acr.docx ← container\n    [Content_Types].xml ← template docx/content-types.xml\n    _rels/.rels ← template docx/rels.xml\n    word/document.xml ← template docx/document.xml.liquid\n    word/_rels/document.xml.rels ← template docx/document.xml.rels\n    word/styles.xml ← template docx/styles.xml\n    docProps/core.xml ← template docx/core.xml.liquid\n  acr.json ← template acr.json.liquid\n\nReferences\n  5 entries, 57.7 KiB\n  references/acr.schema.json (4.7 KiB)\n  references/en-301-549.md (21.4 KiB) — EN 301 549 clauses\n  references/section-508.md (12.6 KiB) — Revised Section 508 criteria\n  references/sources.md (6.3 KiB) — Sources and their terms\n  references/wcag-2.2.md (12.7 KiB) — WCAG 2.2 success criteria\n\nExamples\n  An example is the author's illustration of the inputs, and of the outputs a render of them should produce, kept in the package to test it: not your data.\n  international: en301549, report, section508, wcag (with expected outputs)\n  wcag_only: report, wcag (with expected outputs)\n\nNext:\n  metais read <package> inputs/report.md  — Reads what the input report means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais read <package> inputs/tables.md  — Reads what the input wcag means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais read <package> inputs/tables.md  — Reads what the input section508 means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais read <package> inputs/tables.md  — Reads what the input en301549 means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais schema <package> report --fields  — Lists the fields of the input report, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> wcag --fields  — Lists the fields of the input wcag, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> section508 --fields  — Lists the fields of the input section508, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> en301549 --fields  — Lists the fields of the input en301549, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais grep <package> <pattern> references/  — Searches the package's references.\n  metais validate <package> --data <dir>  — Validates the inputs you gathered in a data directory.\n  metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>  — Renders the outputs from the validated inputs into a new directory.\n  metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>  — Renders an example from generated data, to preview the outputs; generated data is never a real document's facts.\n  metais sample <package> --out <dir>  — Writes generated data as a data directory, data to start from; generated data is never a real document's facts.\n  metais ls <package> examples/international/ -r  — Lists the first example's inputs and expected outputs. An example is the author's illustration of the inputs, and of the outputs a render of them should produce, kept in the package to test it: not your data.\n  metais unpack <package> --out <dir>  — Writes the package's source directory, to edit it and pack it again.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6768f6e0-85fd-4c76-8134-5b4dbe9da1bd","uuid":"125a8716-47e9-4904-9226-39f4e1f5771e","timestamp":"2026-10-22T06:31:59.588Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"/ux/set/bin/metais\nmetais 0.0.0-devel\nspecification 1.0\nAccessibility conformance report — accessibility-conformance-report 1.0.0\nAn accessibility conformance report (ACR) for one product, as a Word document and a JSON copy, from the product's details and one CSV table of criteria for each standard its edition answers.\nMETAIS 1.0, packed, sha256:1d29c7f0c90d4a4ade57ba4cc3ab68e430081dd04bfd15af32c8ba34f889b157\n\nGuide\nThis package writes an accessibility conformance report (ACR): a\ndocument that tells a buyer how far one product meets the accessibility\nstandards they ask about. It writes two files:\n\n- **acr.docx**, the report as a Word document: the product, its vendor,\n  how it was evaluated, the standards it answers, what each conformance\n  level means, and one table per standard with a row per criterion.\n  The WCAG 2.2 table carries W3C's notice for the criterion names and\n  levels it copies, as references/sources.md says.\n- **acr.json**, the same report as JSON for programs, in the shape\n  references/acr.schema.json gives, W3C's notice included. Every render\n  checks the file against that schema before it writes anything.\n\nGather the inputs in this order, as files of one data directory:\n\n1. **report** (report.yaml or report.json): the product, the vendor,\n   the report's date, how the product was evaluated, and the\n   **edition**. Read [what the report details mean](inputs/report.md)\n   first: the edition decides which tables you supply.\n2. **wcag** (wcag.csv): the WCAG 2.2 table, which every edition holds.\n3. **section508** (section508.csv): the Revised Section 508 table, for\n   the section508 and international editions only.\n4. **en301549** (en301549.csv): the EN 301 549 V4.1.1 table, for the en301549\n   and international editions only.\n\nEach table is a CSV file with the header `criterion,conformance,remarks`\nand a row per criterion. [How to fill in a table](inputs/tables.md) says\nwhat each column holds, which criteria to list and how to write the\nremarks, and [what the conformance levels mean](inputs/conformance.md)\nsays which level to choose. What each criterion asks is in the\npackage's references: references/wcag-2.2.md, references/section-508.md\nand references/en-301-549.md, one section per criterion. Search them\nwith `metais grep <package> <number or word> references/`.\n\nBase every level and remark on an evaluation of the real product, and\nsay how it was evaluated in the report's methods. A criterion you did\nnot evaluate is Not Evaluated, never Supports.\n\nThen run `metais validate <package> --data <dir>`, which reports every\nproblem at once: a value that does not fit, a criterion number that does\nnot exist, or a table the edition needs and you left out, or one it\ndoes not hold and you supplied. Fix them all, and run\n`metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>` to write the report.\n\nInputs\n  report — Report details (required; report.yaml, report.yml, report.json)\n    The product, its vendor, the report's date and edition, and how the product was evaluated.\n  wcag — WCAG 2.2 table (required; wcag.csv)\n    One row per WCAG 2.2 success criterion: its number, the product's conformance level and remarks. Every edition holds it.\n  section508 — Revised Section 508 table (optional; section508.csv)\n    One row per criterion of chapters 3 to 6 of the Revised Section 508 Standards. Supplied for the section508 and international editions, and for no other.\n  en301549 — EN 301 549 table (optional; en301549.csv)\n    One row per clause of EN 301 549 V4.1.1 outside its WCAG-based clauses. Supplied for the en301549 and international editions, and for no other.\n\nOutputs\n  acr.docx ← container\n    [Content_Types].xml ← template docx/content-types.xml\n    _rels/.rels ← template docx/rels.xml\n    word/document.xml ← template docx/document.xml.liquid\n    word/_rels/document.xml.rels ← template docx/document.xml.rels\n    word/styles.xml ← template docx/styles.xml\n    docProps/core.xml ← template docx/core.xml.liquid\n  acr.json ← template acr.json.liquid\n\nReferences\n  5 entries, 57.7 KiB\n  references/acr.schema.json (4.7 KiB)\n  references/en-301-549.md (21.4 KiB) — EN 301 549 clauses\n  references/section-508.md (12.6 KiB) — Revised Section 508 criteria\n  references/sources.md (6.3 KiB) — Sources and their terms\n  references/wcag-2.2.md (12.7 KiB) — WCAG 2.2 success criteria\n\nExamples\n  An example is the author's illustration of the inputs, and of the outputs a render of them should produce, kept in the package to test it: not your data.\n  international: en301549, report, section508, wcag (with expected outputs)\n  wcag_only: report, wcag (with expected outputs)\n\nNext:\n  metais read <package> inputs/report.md  — Reads what the input report means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais read <package> inputs/tables.md  — Reads what the input wcag means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais read <package> inputs/tables.md  — Reads what the input section508 means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais read <package> inputs/tables.md  — Reads what the input en301549 means and what good data for it looks like.\n  metais schema <package> report --fields  — Lists the fields of the input report, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> wcag --fields  — Lists the fields of the input wcag, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> section508 --fields  — Lists the fields of the input section508, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais schema <package> en301549 --fields  — Lists the fields of the input en301549, one line each with its type, whether it is required, its constraints and title; without --fields, metais schema prints the whole schema it is validated against.\n  metais grep <package> <pattern> references/  — Searches the package's references.\n  metais validate <package> --data <dir>  — Validates the inputs you gathered in a data directory.\n  metais render <package> --data <dir> --out <dir>  — Renders the outputs from the validated inputs into a new directory.\n  metais render <package> --sample --out <dir>  — Renders an example from generated data, to preview the outputs; generated data is never a real document's facts.\n  metais sample <package> --out <dir>  — Writes generated data as a data directory, data to start from; generated data is never a real document's facts.\n  metais ls <package> examples/international/ -r  — Lists the first example's inputs and expected outputs. An example is the author's illustration of the inputs, and of the outputs a render of them should produce, kept in the package to test it: not your data.\n  metais unpack <package> --out <dir>  — Writes the package's source directory, to edit it and pack it again.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QSgQhq4ce2xbW1ge3x4u7L","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
